          "text": "{\"schema_version\":\"ai-evaluator-v2/1.1\",\"case_id\":\"6-1\",\"candidate_id\":\"candidate_3\",\"action_assessment\":{\"status\":\"defect\",\"summary\":\"Most core activities are represented, but the graph omits the Secretariat's visual inspection and, more importantly, the accounting employee's complaint-resolution activity during commercial audit.\"},\"flow_assessment\":{\"status\":\"preserved\",\"summary\":\"The main matched-action precedence is largely preserved: receipt and accounting setup precede cost-center review, which precedes commercial audit, optional board review, payment instruction, and closure.\"},\"control_flow_assessment\":{\"status\":\"defect\",\"summary\":\"The cost-center review/correction loop is broadly captured, but the commercial-audit complaint branch is missing, so the model incorrectly allows direct continuation toward payment without the required rework path.\"},\"main_error_type\":\"wrong_branching_or_condition\",\"explanation\":\"The source process requires a conditional rework path during commercial audit: if there is a complaint, the accounting employee must resolve it with the supplier, and only after successful completion does payment proceed. In the generated graph, commercial audit always continues directly to the board-review decision and then to payment/closure, with no complaint branch or return path. This changes process behavior by removing a required exception-handling route. The graph otherwise captures the main invoice intake, cost-center approval loop, optional board review, payment, and closure reasonably well, though it also omits the initial visual inspection.\",\"overall_score\":0.76,\"severity\":\"moderate\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"ambiguity_flag\":false,\"ambiguity_explanation\":\"\",\"requires_human_review\":false,\"review_reason\":\"\"}"
        }
